WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:06.475
The humblest instrument on the dash is one of the best navigation checks you have: the depth sounder.

2
00:00:06.725 --> 00:00:10.300
It sends a pulse of sound down, and times the echo.

3
00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:13.325
It shows the depth below the transducer.

4
00:00:13.575 --> 00:00:16.725
Not below the keel, and not from the surface.

5
00:00:16.975 --> 00:00:19.125
Unless you’ve set an offset.

6
00:00:19.375 --> 00:00:21.600
So do you know how yours is set?

7
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.100
Find out.

8
00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:28.550
Two feet of difference matters a great deal on a Florida flat.

9
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.825
Now use it to navigate.

10
00:00:31.075 --> 00:00:35.175
Compare what it reads with the charted depth plus the height of tide.

11
00:00:35.425 --> 00:00:38.675
If they agree, you’re probably where you think you are.

12
00:00:38.925 --> 00:00:41.650
If they don’t, stop and find out why.

13
00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:47.800
In poor visibility, you can follow a depth contour toward a harbor, like following a handrail.

14
00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:50.350
And set the shallow-water alarm.

15
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:53.600
In our skinny water, it’s cheap insurance.
